.TH AI-TEXT-EDITOR 1
.SH NAME
ai-text-editor \- agent-oriented local text and byte editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ai-text-editor
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The editor is a server-owned, revision-aware editor for agents. The server
owns document state, journals, indexes, SQLite metadata, sessions, and jobs.
One server may own multiple isolated file tabs; each tab has separate state,
metadata, and authorization. The initial \fBstart --file PATH\fR creates the
shared endpoint, while \fBopen --endpoint ENDPOINT --file PATH\fR opens or
selects another tab. Closing one tab leaves the endpoint running until the
last tab closes.
The client opens a short-lived request connection and exits after completion.
After \fBopen\fR, the server returns a tab-scoped \fBsession_token\fR. Save it
with \fB--save-session-token PATH\fR and reuse it with \fB--session-token PATH\fR;
requests without the current token are rejected before mutation.
.PP
Mutating commands \fBinsert\fR, \fBreplace\fR, \fBlarge-edit\fR, \fBrestore\fR,
\fBundo\fR, \fBredo\fR, and \fBsave\fR require \fB--expected-revision\fR. A
missing or stale value is refused; read \fBopen\fR or \fBhistory\fR again before
retrying. The server has no project-root jail: the session token grants the
server process's normal filesystem permissions, so keep the endpoint private
and use operating-system isolation when stronger confinement is needed.
.SH STARTING
.IP "server start --file PATH"
Start an editor server and print endpoint/session discovery information.
.IP "open --file PATH"
Open a tab for PATH, starting the server itself when none is running yet.
A PATH that does not exist opens an empty new tab; the file is created at
its path by the first successful \fBsave\fR. A PATH whose parent directory
does not exist is refused with that directory named and nothing created;
\fB--acknowledge-create-parents\fR creates the chain and opens the tab.
\fB--document-mode\fR chooses the mode of the tab being opened
(\fBtext_utf8\fR, \fBraw_bytes\fR, \fBhex_view\fR), whether or not a
workspace is already running. A tab's mode is fixed for its lifetime:
reopening one under a different mode is refused with
\fBdocument_mode_conflict\fR, and \fBclose\fR then \fBopen\fR changes it.
.IP "help"
Print the complete capability and option reference. This file is colocated
with the skill and is available with \fBman -l\fR when binaries are not on PATH.
.PP
Naming \fB--file PATH\fR on any command means "act on that file's tab", and
opening it is part of that: every command opens the tab and starts a server if
none is running, not only \fBopen\fR. The exception is the revision-guarded
commands \fBinsert\fR, \fBreplace\fR, \fBlarge-edit\fR, \fBrestore\fR,
\fBundo\fR, \fBredo\fR and \fBsave\fR on a file with no tab: the revision they
carry cannot have come from a tab that never existed, so they are refused by
name and \fBopen\fR (or any read) comes first.
.PP
Edits change the tab's buffer and journal, never the file, and the bytes reach
disk only when \fBsave\fR succeeds. Mutating and reading responses carry three
state flags, defined in full in \fBreferences/protocol.md\fR: \fBdirty\fR (this
tab's own unsaved edits), \fBdisk_diverged\fR (the file on disk differs from
what this tab last synced with \(em someone else's change) and
\fBexternal_change_pending\fR (a divergence observed and not yet resolved, so
mutations are blocked until \fBresolve-external\fR chooses). An applied edit
also reports the \fBoffset\fR and \fBdelete_len\fR it resolved and the
\fBdeleted\fR bytes, so an edit can be verified from its own answer.
.PP
Refused operations are named on stderr in every
presentation with a code, a message, and recovery choices where they exist;
a \fBfile_mismatch\fR refusal means the request was routed to the tab of a
different file, and opening the named file is the fix.
.PP
A \fBreplace\fR addresses its span three ways, exactly one per call:
\fB--offset\fR with \fB--delete-len\fR in bytes;
\fB--range-start-line\fR with \fB--range-end-line\fR, inclusive and one-based,
the last line's newline included, so a replace with no text deletes the lines
outright; or \fB--range-start-byte\fR with \fB--range-end-byte\fR, half-open \(em
exactly what a search hit reports as \fBbyte_start\fR/\fBbyte_end\fR, so a span
across two hits is those two numbers copied across. \fB--expected-text\fR (or
\fB--expected-bytes-base64\fR) states the bytes the caller believes are at the
span; the server verifies them before deleting anything and refuses
\fBexpected_text_mismatch\fR otherwise. This is not the revision guard and
catches what it cannot: a revision proves the document has not moved since you
read it, not that your length still matches the text there. With
\fB--expected-text\fR and no \fB--delete-len\fR, its own length is the length.
.PP
\fB--match-id ID\fR addresses a \fBreplace\fR by a search hit's own id
instead of a range: it resolves to that hit's exact span and carries its own
content guard, refusing \fBmatch_id_stale\fR if the document moved under it.
It takes no range key, \fB--offset\fR, or \fB--expected-text\fR alongside it,
and \fBinsert\fR refuses it as \fBedit_range_unsupported\fR, the same as a
range. \fBsearch --preview-lines N\fR shrinks a large hit's shown contents to
its first and last N lines; \fBbyte_start\fR/\fBbyte_end\fR, and so
\fB--match-id\fR, stay exact regardless.
.PP
For TCP startup use exactly one of \fB--auth-token TOKEN\fR or an owner-only
\fB--auth-token-file PATH\fR. Clients pass \fB--auth-token TOKEN\fR or reuse a
saved session token.
.PP
\fB--session ID\fR and \fB--agent ID\fR resolve server-registered candidates
from the private \fBsessions.json\fR registry. Resolution is newest-first but
refuses ambiguity and unreachable stale records; it never connects to or
impersonates a different server. Combined with \fB--file PATH\fR it may
autostart a fresh server, as \fBopen\fR does, and that server registers itself
under the named identity; identity resolution alone, with no file, starts
nothing.
.SH RESPONSE SIZE
Every command takes \fB--verbosity 0|1|2|3\fR, and 1 is the default. Level 0 is
the command's own result and the \fBtab_id\fR that produced it, and nothing
else. Level 1 adds what verification and the next step need: \fBrevision\fR,
\fBdirty\fR, \fBdisk_diverged\fR, \fBexternal_change_pending\fR, the tab's
\fBmode\fR, the resolved edit span, completeness, and a search's pager key and
count. Level 2 adds navigation: cursors, byte windows, index block paging and
undo depths. Level 3 is everything.
.PP
Three rules the levels do not bend: every level carries the command's own
result and names its tab; a refusal keeps its whole code, message and recovery
choices at every level; and \fBcapabilities\fR and \fBresources\fR ignore the
level, their payload being metadata by definition. A level outside 0-3 is
refused with \fBverbosity_invalid\fR rather than clamped.
.PP
The default is 1 and not 0 by deliberate choice: level 0 cannot carry a
revision, and every mutation's guard requires one, so a level-0 default would
break the revision contract for any caller that then tried to edit.
.SH ADDRESSING A TAB
Every response reports a \fBtab_id\fR, and that id is addressing enough on its
own for every command: \fB--tab-id ID\fR needs no \fB--file\fR and no
\fB--endpoint\fR. An id naming no tab is refused with \fBtab_unknown\fR and
the open tabs listed; it never falls through to another tab.
.PP
\fB--tab-path FRAGMENT\fR is the recovery when the id is lost: a filename, or
a trailing run of path components, naming an open tab. Matching is on path
components rather than substrings, so \fBport.txt\fR does not name
\fBreport.txt\fR. A fragment naming several tabs is refused with
\fBtab_ambiguous\fR and the candidates with their ids; one naming none with
\fBtab_unmatched\fR and the open tabs. Neither is a guess and neither is a
bare refusal.
.PP
A command naming nothing at all runs on the \fIfocused\fR tab: the tab the
last successful call under this identity was served by. \fBopen\fR focuses the
tab it opens, and a refused call never moves the focus. With no focus and
nothing named, the command is refused with all four forms named.
.PP
Every response names the tab it answered. That is the safety half of allowing
an unmarked command: read \fBtab_id\fR from the answer rather than assuming
the focus is where you left it.
.PP
An argument the command does not read is refused with
\fBunknown_argument\fR, and the refusal lists what that command does accept.
The check is per command, so an argument valid on a \fIdifferent\fR command is
refused too rather than silently ignored.
.SH MODES
Text, raw bytes, and hex view are explicit document modes. Search modes are
explicit: exact text/bytes, wildcard, shell wildcard, path wildcard, Rust or
PCRE2 regex, and six fuzzy strategies. Structured output is the default;
text, paging, and streaming are explicit presentations. The
\fBexact_bytes\fR mode decodes its query as base64-encoded bytes, whether the
query arrives in \fB--query\fR or \fB--query-base64\fR; plain text belongs in
\fBexact_text\fR. Text-mode search results carry line/column coordinates
and the absolute \fBbyte_start\fR/\fBbyte_end\fR offsets that
\fBinsert\fR and \fBreplace\fR consume.
.PP
Every text mode matches within one line: the document is split on newlines and
each line is matched without its terminator. A query containing a newline is
therefore refused with \fBsearch_query_crosses_lines\fR rather than answered
with a zero, which would be indistinguishable from absent text.
\fBexact_bytes\fR is the mode that spans lines. A text search that finds nothing
and whose query contains HTML entities carries a note, and the count it would
have found, when the same query unescaped does match.
.PP
\fBcapabilities\fR is read-only machine-readable discovery for these modes,
coordinate units, defaults, resource limits, and transports. Use it before
choosing behavior that is not already known to the agent.
.PP
Pass \fB--normalize-nfc\fR when starting the server to opt into normalized text
presentation. Stored-byte coordinates remain distinct from normalized text.
The \fBrestore\fR command turns normalized presentation off when restoration is
lossless and refuses with a restoration conflict after a lossy edit.
.SH SAFETY
External changes alert at the next interaction. Backup, reload, merge, keep, and
force-save require the agent to choose the operation; force-save requires an
acknowledgement. External bytes can be preserved atomically as PATH.back by
default. Backup failure or collision prevents the resolution. Closing a tab
asks whether its journal is preserved or explicitly cleaned. TCP is a private
loopback fallback; startup requires --auth-token or --auth-token-file. Each connection performs a
fresh HMAC challenge/proof exchange over a nonce and server generation; the
secret is not placed in the editor request. The challenge nonce and request ID
must match, and failed proofs are rejected before the editor handler runs.
An owner-only auth-token-file is reread per TCP connection to permit rotation.
Never expose the endpoint publicly.
.PP
Unix endpoint discovery records are atomic and include the owning PID and
server generation. Graceful shutdown removes the active record. After a crash
or a plain kill, an unreachable socket is retained as stale evidence. A
replacement server reclaims it automatically when the recorded owner PID is
demonstrably gone (the next \fBopen\fR does this through autostart); when the
owner is still alive or cannot be determined, startup refuses to impersonate
it unless \fB--takeover-stale-endpoint\fR is explicitly supplied. The old
record is preserved with a \fBstale-\fR suffix during takeover.
.PP
On Windows, where no usable Unix socket exists, \fBopen\fR autostarts a
loopback-TCP server on an OS-assigned ephemeral port with a per-start private
authentication token, and discovers it through the same per-file endpoint
record the Unix transport uses. Every command works unchanged over that
fallback; the secret is kept in a token file, never on the command line.
.PP
\fBsave-as\fR atomically creates a new target with the current working view and
never overwrites an existing target or changes the active tab path.
.PP
Closing is explicit: run \fBclose\fR without a journal action to receive a
decision prompt, then repeat with \fB--journal-action preserve\fR or
\fB--journal-action clean\fR. Cleaning deletes the tab journal and metadata.
.SH FILE DISCOVERY
The installed package may keep the binaries outside PATH. Discover this manual
beside the skill and use \fBman -l /path/to/ai-text-editor.1\fR.
.SH LARGE FILES
Files above 256 MiB use bounded file-backed reads and sparse index scans.
The protocol caps frames at 8 MiB; large raw reads are capped at 4 MiB before
base64 framing, and oversized result windows must be paged.
Large searches persist matches incrementally in bounded SQLite chunks and keep
only the preview in memory; use the returned pager key with \fBpage\fR to fetch
bounded result slices.
Startup records only a bounded prefix index for a large tab. Use the
index operation for an explicit full scan and inspect the reported coverage.
Index output returns four blocks by default; use \fB--offset\fR and
\fB--limit\fR to page the persisted block list. The response reports its total
block count and returned window.
Use \fB--offset\fR and \fB--length\fR for reads. Ordinary mutations are
refused; start a job and use \fBlarge-edit\fR with
\fB--acknowledge-large-edit\fR to stream an explicitly accepted rewrite.
Large edits retain file-backed before/after snapshots for undo and redo; clean
the tab journal when those snapshots are no longer needed.
Large-tab external backup streams the current file to an atomic \fB.back\fR
copy, and reload refreshes metadata and lazy index coverage. Merge and
force-save require an acknowledged bounded rewrite job.
.PP
Short-lived requests may run concurrently. A streamed text read is a revision
snapshot; when a write lands during delivery, the server emits the restart
delimiter and a fresh stream. Consumers must discard frames before that
delimiter.
.SH HISTORY AND CURSORS
\fBhistory\fR reports undo and redo depths without exposing another tab.
\fBjump-points\fR reports this file's outbound CodeGraph references (calls,
imports, instantiations, and similar), recomputed server-side at open and
after every save by reading CodeGraph's own SQLite index directly. Each entry
names the referring line and column, the edge kind, and the target file, line,
name and kind it points to. The response's \fBrevision\fR is the tab revision
they were computed at; \fBstale: true\fR means the tab has since been edited
and \fBsave\fR would recompute them. \fBjump_points: null\fR means CodeGraph is
not enabled for this project, or its index is not in a shape this reader
supports -- not that the file has no references.
\fBresources\fR reports available memory, estimated server overhead, the
recommended working set, and the active large-file threshold.
Mutation responses include every numeric cursor position. The \fBcursor\fR
command moves numbered cursors: \fB--id N\fR selects which cursor to move
(default 0; a cursor is created by its first move). \fBhome\fR and
\fBend\fR anchor to the first and last column of the \fIcurrent line\fR, not
the document's start or end; \fBnext_word\fR and \fBprevious_word\fR step
words; \fBpage_up\fR and \fBpage_down\fR move \fB--page-lines N\fR lines
(default 40). Text coordinates use
one-based lines and zero-based Unicode scalar columns; raw and hex modes use
byte offsets.
.PP
Pass \fB--wrap-width N\fR to receive the corresponding one-based visual row and
zero-based wrapped-row column. Pass \fB--visual\fR with \fB--line\fR and
\fB--column\fR to interpret the input as wrapped coordinates; \fBN\fR must be
positive. Stored cursors and mutation coordinates remain logical, and the
response includes both logical and visual positions.
.PP
All large-file text searches require an explicit inclusive line range with
\fB--range-start-line\fR and \fB--range-end-line\fR. Exact byte searches use
the half-open \fB--range-start-byte\fR/\fB--range-end-byte\fR range. Unbounded
scans are refused.
.PP
Bounded reads take the same pair of bounds: a text read honors
\fB--range-start-line\fR/\fB--range-end-line\fR as an inclusive window, and a
raw or hex read honors \fB--range-start-byte\fR/\fB--range-end-byte\fR as a
half-open window. A bound the tab cannot honor \-\- a line range on a raw tab,
a byte range on a text tab, or a range combined with an offset window \-\- is
refused with the offending flag named.
.PP
Use \fBbegin-transaction\fR and \fBend-transaction\fR to group ordinary edits
into one undo step. Each edit remains in the recovery journal.
.SH JOBS
Use \fBjob-start\fR, \fBjob-poll\fR, \fBjob-progress\fR, \fBjob-complete\fR,
\fBjob-cancel\fR, \fBjob-transfer\fR, and \fBjob-release\fR to manage a
server-side lifecycle record. A job record does not execute work itself. The
driving agent is responsible for the work, truthful progress, and cleanup;
detached jobs survive a client disconnect for the configured retention.
.SH SEE ALSO
The colocated \fBreferences/protocol.md\fR contains coordinate, search,
stream, job, journal, and external-change contracts.
